'use strict';
const lessons = [
{module:1,title:'Meet your first database',time:8,lab:'explore',intro:'You already know how to browse movies. Now discover the organised data behind that screen.',goal:'Explain the difference between data, a database, and a DBMS.',cards:[['Start with one fact','“Drishyam was released in 2013” is data: a fact we can record. Store related facts about movies, genres, and reviews together and you have a database.'],['Who looks after the data?','A <b>DBMS</b> is software that stores, retrieves, and protects the database. In your CineSuper assignment, PostgreSQL is the DBMS provided through Supabase. CineSuper is the application that asks it for data.'],['Why not three spreadsheets?','Separate files can repeat facts and disagree. A DBMS can enforce shared rules, control access, support simultaneous users, and recover from failures. It does not automatically fix a poorly designed schema.']],question:'What is the DBMS in the CineSuper assignment?',options:['The CineSuper movie cards','PostgreSQL','The list of movie titles'],answer:1,why:'PostgreSQL manages the data. The cards are the application’s display; the titles are data.',hint:'Look for the software responsible for storing and enforcing rules on the data.'},
{module:1,title:'Behind the movie screen',time:10,lab:'architecture',intro:'A student sees a movie card. A developer sees tables. A DBMS sees stored pages. These are different views of the same data.',goal:'Distinguish abstraction levels, application tiers, and common data models.',cards:[['Three levels of abstraction','<b>External / view:</b> the facts a particular user needs. <b>Logical / conceptual:</b> tables, attributes, relationships, and rules. <b>Physical:</b> files, pages, and indexes used for storage. Physical data independence lets storage change without changing the logical design. Logical independence means changing the logical design without forcing changes to user views.'],['Levels are not application tiers','A three-tier application has a presentation layer, application/API layer, and database layer. CineSuper’s browser sends requests through Supabase’s API to PostgreSQL. Inside a DBMS, the query processor interprets requests, the storage manager accesses data, and the transaction manager coordinates safe changes.'],['A model is a way to describe data','The relational model represents facts in tables. ER modelling describes entities and relationships before implementation. Hierarchical models use a tree; network models use linked records. Document models group fields in documents; graph models emphasise nodes and edges. For CineSuper we use ER for design and relations for storage.']],question:'Adding an index without changing the movie table’s columns is an example of…',options:['Changing the user’s review','Physical data independence','Creating a new entity'],answer:1,why:'The storage/access strategy changes while the logical table remains the same.',hint:'Which abstraction level contains indexes?'},
{module:1,title:'Keys keep the story straight',time:12,lab:'constraints',intro:'Two films can share a title. A review must point to the right one. Keys give every record a reliable identity.',goal:'Identify primary, candidate, composite, and foreign keys; explain integrity constraints.',cards:[['Identity, not just a familiar name','A <b>superkey</b> is any set of attributes that uniquely identifies a row. A <b>candidate key</b> is a minimal superkey: remove any part and uniqueness is no longer guaranteed. One candidate key is chosen as the <b>primary key</b>; others are alternate keys. A composite key has multiple attributes. In movies, id is the primary key. A title is not guaranteed unique.'],['A foreign key makes a promise','reviews.movie_id refers to movies.id. Every non-null foreign key must match an existing referenced key. This is referential integrity. A foreign key can repeat: many reviews can point to the same movie. In the assignment, review.movie_id is required; movie.genre_id is optional.'],['Rules prevent impossible facts','Primary keys are unique and non-null (entity integrity). A domain limits allowed values; reviews.rating is an integer from 1 to 5. NOT NULL requires a value; UNIQUE prevents duplicates. genres.name is both UNIQUE and NOT NULL, so it is also a candidate key. A CHECK alone does not necessarily forbid NULL; use NOT NULL when a value is compulsory.']],question:'Why can two reviews contain the same movie_id?',options:['Foreign keys must always be unique','Both reviews can refer to the same movie','The primary key has stopped working'],answer:1,why:'The reviews have different primary-key ids. Their foreign key identifies the movie, so it may repeat.',hint:'Think of two students reviewing Drishyam.'},
{module:1,title:'Draw the relationships',time:12,lab:'er',intro:'Before writing a table, describe the world. CineSuper has movies, genres, and reviews—and clear links between them.',goal:'Recognise entities, attributes, cardinality, and participation in an ER model.',cards:[['Things, details, connections','A <b>Movie</b> is an entity type. One particular movie is an entity instance. Title and language are attributes. “A Movie receives a Review” is a relationship. An ER diagram makes these ideas visible before choosing storage details.'],['How many can connect?','Cardinality describes maximum counts: one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many. In this assignment, one genre can categorise many movies, while each movie has at most one genre. One movie can receive many reviews; each review belongs to one movie.'],['Can the connection be missing?','Participation describes the minimum. A movie can have zero reviews; a review must have one movie (total participation of Review). movies.genre_id can be NULL, so a movie may have no genre. A genre may have no movies. Cardinality and optionality come from business rules, not from how many rows happen to exist today.']],question:'Which statement matches the actual CineSuper schema?',options:['Every movie must have at least one review','Every review belongs to exactly one movie','Every genre must contain exactly one movie'],answer:1,why:'reviews.movie_id is a required foreign key. A movie can exist before anyone reviews it.',hint:'The required foreign key is on the reviews table.'},
{module:1,title:'Model a little more of the world',time:12,lab:'eer',intro:'Sometimes an ordinary entity needs a richer description. Extend CineSuper on paper to explore EER concepts.',goal:'Explain attribute types, weak entities, and specialisation/generalisation.',cards:[['Attributes have different shapes','A simple attribute is not split further for the current purpose, such as rating. A composite attribute can be divided, such as a reviewer address into city and postcode. A multivalued attribute has several values, such as a movie’s audio languages. A derived attribute is calculated, such as a movie’s average rating from reviews. These are modelling extensions, not extra columns in the assignment.'],['Subtypes inherit a common identity','Imagine a CineSuper Content entity with Movie and Series subtypes. This is <b>specialisation</b>; generalisation works in the reverse direction. A disjoint rule allows only one subtype; overlapping allows more than one. Total specialisation requires every Content to be in a subtype; partial permits Content in neither. Declare the business rule before drawing it.'],['A weak entity needs its owner','In a practice extension, Episode is identified by (series_id, episode_no), assuming numbering is unique within each series. episode_no alone is a partial key. The owner’s key plus the partial key identifies the weak entity. The actual reviews table has its own id, so it is not a weak entity merely because it has a foreign key.']],question:'Episode numbers restart at 1 for each series. Which key identifies an episode in this extension?',options:['episode_no alone','series_id alone','(series_id, episode_no)'],answer:2,why:'Neither part is sufficient alone. Together they identify an episode under the stated numbering rule.',hint:'Two different series can both have episode 1.'},
{module:1,title:'Turn your ER model into tables',time:12,lab:'mapping',intro:'Your model is a plan. Mapping turns that plan into relations the DBMS can store.',goal:'Map entities and 1:1, 1:N, M:N, multivalued, weak, and subtype structures to relations.',cards:[['Start with entities and one-to-many links','Create a table for each strong entity, flatten composite attributes, and select a primary key. For Genre 1:N Movie, put genre_id on the many side: movies. For Movie 1:N Review, put movie_id in reviews. Total participation on that side requires NOT NULL.'],['Other links need other rules','For 1:1, a foreign key plus UNIQUE can enforce at most one matching row; place it thoughtfully, often on a totally participating side. For M:N, create a bridge table with both foreign keys. If CineSuper later permits multiple genres per movie, use MovieGenre(movie_id, genre_id) with a composite primary key. That is an extension: today the assignment permits at most one genre per movie.'],['Map richer structures','Move a multivalued attribute into a separate table, such as MovieAudio(movie_id, language). Map a weak entity with its owner key and partial key. A common subtype mapping uses Content(id, title), Movie(id, duration), and Series(id, seasons), where each subtype id is both its primary key and a foreign key to Content. Additional rules may be needed to enforce total or disjoint specialisation.']],question:'If CineSuper adds a many-to-many movie–genre relationship, what should you create?',options:['A comma-separated list in movies.genre_id','A MovieGenre bridge with movie_id and genre_id','One table for each individual movie'],answer:1,why:'Each bridge row stores one movie–genre association, and the composite key prevents duplicate pairs.',hint:'Represent one association per row.'},
{module:2,title:'Think in rows and columns',time:12,lab:'algebra',intro:'Relational algebra is a small toolkit for asking questions about tables. Start with “which rows?” and “which columns?”',goal:'Distinguish schema from instance and apply selection, projection, and rename.',cards:[['A relation is a set of tuples','An attribute is a named column; a tuple is a row; a domain is the allowed set of values for an attribute. A schema describes the structure, such as movies(id, title, language, genre_id). An instance is the current set of rows. Mathematical relations contain no duplicate tuples and have no inherent row order. SQL tables/results can behave differently.'],['Selection filters rows; projection chooses columns','<b>Selection σ</b> keeps tuples satisfying a condition: σ language="Tamil" (movies). <b>Projection π</b> keeps chosen attributes: π language (movies). Under set semantics, projection removes duplicate results. Combine them to ask for titles of Tamil movies.'],['Rename makes expressions clear','<b>Rename ρ</b> changes the name of a relation or its attributes in an expression. It helps distinguish two copies of a relation in a self-join. Operators return relations too, so the output of one operation can become the input of the next. This property is called closure.']],question:'Which operation returns the distinct languages available in CineSuper?',options:['Select rows where language = Tamil','Project the language attribute','Rename movies to languages'],answer:1,why:'Projection chooses the language column and eliminates duplicates under relational algebra’s set semantics.',hint:'You want a column, not a filtered group of rows.'},
{module:2,title:'Combine tables, discover answers',time:14,lab:'sets',intro:'Useful questions often need more than one table. Join movies to genres, or compare two sets of movie IDs.',goal:'Apply union, difference, intersection, product, join, and division.',cards:[['Combine compatible sets','Union (∪) returns tuples in either relation; intersection (∩) returns tuples in both; difference (−) returns tuples in the left but not the right. These operations require union-compatible relations: corresponding attributes have compatible domains and the same number of attributes. Set union removes duplicates.'],['Product first; matching second','A Cartesian product (×) pairs every row on the left with every row on the right. A theta join keeps pairs satisfying a condition. Join movies.genre_id = genres.id to attach genre names. A natural join matches all same-named attributes automatically; it is dangerous here because both tables have an id column with different meanings. Use the intended join condition.'],['Division asks “for every”','For a practice relation Watched(viewer, movie_id) and a required set Required(movie_id), Watched ÷ Required returns viewers who watched every required movie. Union, difference, Cartesian product, selection, projection, and rename form a basic operator set; joins, intersection, and division can be expressed using them.']],question:'How should movies and genres be joined in CineSuper?',options:['movies.id = genres.id','movies.genre_id = genres.id','movies.title = genres.name'],answer:1,why:'The foreign key movies.genre_id refers to the primary key genres.id. The two id columns describe different entities.',hint:'Follow the foreign key, not matching column names.'},
{module:2,title:'Find what determines what',time:14,lab:'fd',intro:'If you know a movie’s ID, you can find its title. That one-way guarantee is a functional dependency.',goal:'Interpret functional dependencies and compute attribute closure.',cards:[['A rule across all valid data','X → Y means that any two tuples agreeing on X must agree on Y. X is the determinant. In movies, id → title, language, genre_id. A title does not necessarily determine id: remakes and unrelated films can share a name. A small sample can disprove a dependency with a counterexample, but cannot prove it is a permanent business rule.'],['Follow the arrows','In a teaching relation MovieInfo(movie_id, title, genre_id, genre_name), assume movie_id → title, genre_id and genre_id → genre_name. Then movie_id → genre_name by transitivity. Reflexivity says X → any subset of X. Augmentation says X → Y implies XZ → YZ. These rules help derive further dependencies.'],['Closure tells you what you can find','X⁺ is the set of all attributes determined by X under the stated dependencies. Start with X, repeatedly add the right side of every dependency whose left side is already known, and stop when nothing changes. X is a superkey if its closure contains every attribute; it is a candidate key if no proper subset does so.']],question:'Under the given MovieInfo dependencies, what is {genre_id}⁺?',options:['genre_id and genre_name','Every attribute','movie_id and title only'],answer:0,why:'genre_id determines genre_name, but one genre can have many movies. It does not determine movie_id or title.',hint:'Only follow arrows whose left-hand attributes you already have.'},
{module:2,title:'One fact in each cell · 1NF',time:12,lab:'normal1',intro:'A cell containing “5, 4” looks convenient. But which reviewer gave which rating? Start by making each fact individually accessible.',goal:'Recognise repeating groups and insertion, update, and deletion anomalies.',cards:[['First normal form','A relation is in 1NF when each attribute value is atomic for the chosen domain and there are no repeating groups. In this teaching design, each rating value is one integer, not a list. Put each review in its own row. Atomic does not mean a value can never be subdivided in any context; the domain and intended operations matter.'],['Repeating facts cause anomalies','If every review row repeats the movie title, changing the title requires multiple updates (update anomaly). If a movie can only be recorded with a review, you cannot add an unreviewed movie cleanly (insertion anomaly). Deleting the last review might erase the only movie information (deletion anomaly).'],['1NF is a starting point','Separate rows solve repeating groups, but repeated movie facts may remain. Normalisation uses dependencies to decide which facts belong together. Later steps separate movie facts from review facts; merely adding an id does not remove the underlying dependencies or repetition.']],question:'Which design matches the chosen integer domain for rating and satisfies this 1NF requirement?',options:['One cell containing “5, 4, 3”','Columns rating1, rating2, rating3','One review row per rating'],answer:2,why:'Each review has its own row and each rating cell stores one integer. Repeating numbered columns are a repeating group.',hint:'Think about adding a fourth or fifth review without changing the columns.'},
{module:2,title:'Remove partial dependencies · 2NF',time:14,lab:'normal2',intro:'Some facts describe a movie. Others describe a particular review. A composite key helps expose the difference.',goal:'Identify partial dependencies and decompose a 1NF relation into 2NF.',cards:[['Use an explicit teaching assumption','Consider ReviewSheet(movie_id, reviewer, title, rating). For this exercise only, assume one review per reviewer per movie, and unique reviewer names. The candidate key is (movie_id, reviewer). Dependencies: movie_id → title and (movie_id, reviewer) → rating. The real CineSuper schema uses a review id and does not impose this one-review rule.'],['Second normal form','A relation is in 2NF when it is in 1NF and no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of any candidate key. A prime attribute belongs to at least one candidate key. Here title is non-prime and depends only on movie_id, part of the composite key. That is a partial dependency.'],['Put the movie fact with the movie','Split into Movie(movie_id, title) and Review(movie_id, reviewer, rating). Title is now stored once per movie. In this example, joining on movie_id reconstructs the original facts without extra tuples. A relation whose candidate keys all have one attribute has no partial-key dependencies, but may still fail 3NF.']],question:'Why does ReviewSheet fail 2NF under the stated assumptions?',options:['rating depends on the full key','title depends only on movie_id, part of a candidate key','The table has too many rows'],answer:1,why:'The non-prime attribute title depends on a proper subset of the candidate key (movie_id, reviewer).',hint:'Which fact can be found without knowing the reviewer?'},
{module:2,title:'Remove the extra hop · 3NF',time:14,lab:'normal3',intro:'A movie points to a genre, and the genre has a name. Storing the name beside every movie repeats a separate fact.',goal:'Explain transitive dependencies and apply the formal 3NF condition.',cards:[['Find the chain','In MovieInfo(movie_id, title, genre_id, genre_name), assume movie_id is the only candidate key and genre names are not assumed unique in this teaching relation. The dependencies are movie_id → title, genre_id and genre_id → genre_name. So the movie key determines genre_name through genre_id.'],['Third normal form','For every non-trivial dependency X → A, 3NF requires X to be a superkey or A to be prime (part of some candidate key). “Non-trivial” means A is not already in X. Here genre_id is not a superkey of MovieInfo, and genre_name is non-prime, so the dependency violates 3NF. The useful beginner shortcut is to remove non-key facts that depend on other non-key facts.'],['Give each fact a home','Decompose into Movies(movie_id, title, genre_id) and Genres(genre_id, genre_name). One genre name now has one home. This mirrors the actual assignment’s separate genres table, where name is additionally unique. Normal form claims depend on the declared dependencies, not only on the data currently visible.']],question:'Which dependency causes the 3NF violation in MovieInfo?',options:['movie_id → title','genre_id → genre_name','movie_id → genre_id'],answer:1,why:'genre_id is not a superkey of MovieInfo, and genre_name is not a prime attribute under the stated assumptions.',hint:'Find the determinant that cannot identify a whole MovieInfo row.'},
{module:2,title:'A stronger rule · BCNF',time:16,lab:'bcnf',intro:'Even a 3NF relation can repeat facts. BCNF checks every determinant—and decomposition must preserve meaning.',goal:'Distinguish 3NF from BCNF, and evaluate lossless joins and dependency preservation.',cards:[['A small CineSuper extension','Suppose CuratorAssignment(movie, curator, genre) records curators choosing a genre for a movie. Assume (movie, genre) → curator and curator → genre: each curator specialises in one genre. Candidate keys are (movie, genre) and (movie, curator). All three attributes are prime, so the relation satisfies 3NF. These are practice rules, not existing CineSuper tables.'],['Boyce–Codd normal form','BCNF requires the determinant of every non-trivial functional dependency to be a superkey. curator → genre violates BCNF because a curator can work on several movies. Split into CuratorGenre(curator, genre) and MovieCurator(movie, curator). Both are in BCNF under the projected dependencies.'],['A correct split must join back correctly','A decomposition is lossless if joining its projections reconstructs exactly the original relation for every valid instance. For a binary split, a useful FD test is that the common attributes determine all attributes of at least one component. Here curator determines CuratorGenre, so the split is lossless. But (movie, genre) → curator is not enforceable within either component alone: this split loses dependency preservation. A dependency-preserving, lossless 3NF design may therefore be preferable.']],question:'Why does curator → genre violate BCNF in CuratorAssignment?',options:['genre is a prime attribute','curator does not determine the whole relation','There are only three columns'],answer:1,why:'curator is not a superkey: it does not determine movie. BCNF has no prime-attribute exception.',hint:'BCNF asks whether every non-trivial determinant is a superkey.'},
{module:2,title:'Design decisions & final challenge',time:14,lab:'capstone',intro:'A good database balances clear facts, reliable rules, and useful performance. Bring the two modules together.',goal:'Weigh denormalisation trade-offs and justify a complete CineSuper design.',cards:[['Normalise first; measure before duplicating','Normalisation reduces avoidable repetition and anomalies. More relations can mean more joins. Denormalisation intentionally stores redundant data to improve a measured workload, but adds storage and maintenance work. It is a trade-off, not a higher normal form.'],['A live calculation is not the same as a stored copy','CineSuper’s movie_ratings view calculates average ratings and review counts from underlying data. An ordinary view stores a query definition, not an independently maintained result. A stored average or materialised result can speed reads but needs a refresh/update strategy and may become stale. Decide how fresh a value must be before choosing it.'],['Explain your choices','For the existing assignment: genres owns genre names; movies owns movie details and an optional genre_id; reviews owns each rating and its required movie_id. Use constraints for identity and valid values. For each proposed new fact, ask what determines it, where it belongs, and whether a split joins back without creating false facts.']],question:'What is the main risk of storing avg_rating directly in movies as a separate value?',options:['The value may become stale when reviews change','Movies can no longer have a primary key','All joins become impossible'],answer:0,why:'A stored aggregate duplicates information derivable from reviews, so changes must keep it synchronised.',hint:'What happens immediately after a new review is added?'}
];
const movies=[{id:1,title:'Drishyam',release_year:2013,language:'Malayalam',genre_id:3},{id:2,title:'Premam',release_year:2015,language:'Malayalam',genre_id:2},{id:3,title:'Bangalore Days',release_year:2014,language:'Malayalam',genre_id:4},{id:4,title:'Minnal Murali',release_year:2021,language:'Malayalam',genre_id:1},{id:6,title:'Vikram',release_year:2022,language:'Tamil',genre_id:1},{id:11,title:'Inception',release_year:2010,language:'English',genre_id:5}];
const genres=[{id:1,name:'Action'},{id:2,name:'Drama'},{id:3,name:'Thriller'},{id:4,name:'Comedy'},{id:5,name:'Sci-Fi'}];
const reviews=[{id:1,movie_id:1,reviewer_name:'Anjali',rating:5},{id:2,movie_id:1,reviewer_name:'Ravi',rating:4},{id:3,movie_id:6,reviewer_name:'Meera',rating:5}];
const glossary={
'Attribute':'A named property or column, such as movies.title.',
'BCNF':'For every non-trivial functional dependency, the determinant must be a superkey.',
'Candidate key':'A minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple.',
'Cardinality':'The maximum number of related entities: one or many.',
'Composite key':'A key made from more than one attribute.',
'Database':'An organised collection of related data.',
'DBMS':'Software that manages a database; CineSuper uses PostgreSQL through Supabase.',
'Decomposition':'Splitting a relation into smaller relations, with attention to losslessness and dependency preservation.',
'Denormalisation':'Deliberately introducing redundancy for a measured benefit, with a strategy to keep copies consistent.',
'Dependency preservation':'All original functional dependencies can be enforced using the dependencies on individual decomposed relations, without joining them.',
'Determinant':'The attributes on the left of a functional dependency.',
'Domain':'The permitted values for an attribute, such as integer ratings from 1 to 5.',
'Entity':'A distinguishable thing being modelled, such as a particular movie.',
'Foreign key':'Attributes whose non-null values must match a referenced key in another (or the same) table.',
'Functional dependency':'X → Y: tuples agreeing on X must agree on Y in every valid instance.',
'Instance':'The data in a database or relation at a particular time.',
'Join':'Combines related tuples from relations using a matching condition.',
'Lossless join':'Joining the decomposed relations gives exactly the original relation, without missing or invented tuples.',
'Normalisation':'Organising relations using dependencies to reduce redundancy and anomalies.',
'NULL':'A marker for missing, unknown, or inapplicable information; it is not zero or an empty string.',
'Participation':'Whether an entity must take part in a relationship (total) or may not (partial).',
'Primary key':'The candidate key chosen as the main identifier; it is unique and non-null.',
'Prime attribute':'An attribute that belongs to at least one candidate key.',
'Projection π':'Chooses attributes from a relation and eliminates duplicate tuples under set semantics.',
'Relation':'A set of tuples with named attributes; commonly represented as a table.',
'Schema':'The structure of a database or relation, including attributes and constraints.',
'Selection σ':'Keeps tuples satisfying a condition.',
'Superkey':'Any attribute set that uniquely identifies a tuple; it may contain unnecessary attributes.',
'Tuple':'A single row in a relation.',
'Weak entity':'An entity identified using an owner’s key together with its own partial key.',
'1NF':'Values are atomic within their domains and there are no repeating groups.',
'2NF':'1NF, with no non-prime attribute depending on a proper subset of any candidate key.',
'3NF':'For every non-trivial X → A, either X is a superkey or A is prime.'};
